hadiths[3]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2885","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr, who said: Narrated to us by Isma'il, meaning the son of Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad, who is the son of Abu Harmalah, from Kurayb, the freed slave of Ibn Abbas. Kurayb said: Abdullah bin Abbas informed me that Al-Fadl informed him that the Prophet (ﷺ) continued to recite the Talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrah.\n\nAbu Bakr (the commentator) said: I"
"2886","It was narrated from Ibn Mas‘ūd (RA) that he said:\n*\I observed the Prophet (ﷺ) continuously reciting the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-‘Aqabah with the first pebble.\""*\n\nThis was narrated to us by ‘Alī ibn Ḥujr"," who said: Sharīk informed us"," from ‘Āmir"," from Abū Wā’il"," from ‘Abdullāh (RA)"," who said:\n*\""I observed the Prophet (ﷺ) continuously reciting the talbiyah until he stoned Jamrat al-‘Aqabah with the first pebble.\""*\n\nAbū Bakr (ibn Abī Shaybah) said:\n*Some scholars may think that this report indicates that the Prophet (ﷺ) would stop reciting the talbiyah upon throwing the first pebble at Jamrat al-‘Aqabah. However"," in my view"," this falls under the category of matters where a command or prohibition in a report may be time-bound"," but the mention of the time does not imply that the command or prohibition ceases after that time.*\n\n*For example"," the Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited prayer after the dawn (Fajr) prayer until the sun has fully risen. This does not mean that once the sun has fully risen"," prayer becomes permissible at sunrise. Rather"," the Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited praying at the exact moment of sunrise because the sun rises between the horns of Satan"," and he forbade prayer at that time. He then said"," ‘When it has risen"," leave it","’ directing them to understand that prayer at sunrise is not permissible until the sun has fully risen.*\n\n*I have expounded on many such cases in my authored works. The proof for the correctness of this interpretation is the explicit hadith that we were narrated...\""*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"""
"2887","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Hafs al-Shaybani: Narrated to us Hafs ibn Ghiyath: Narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad: On the authority of his father: On the authority of 'Ali ibn Husayn: On the authority of Ibn 'Abbas: On the authority of his brother al-Fadl who said:\n\n\I performed the *ifadah* (departure) with the Prophet (ﷺ) at 'Arafat. He continued to recite the *talbiyah* until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah"," saying *Allahu Akbar* with every pebble. Then he discontinued the *talbiyah* with the last pebble.\""\n\nAbu Bakr (the commentator) said: \""This narration explicitly states that he discontinued the *talbiyah* with the last pebble"," not with the first one. If some seekers of knowledge do not understand this type of clarification that we have mentioned regarding the timing"," then the essence of these two narrations is that if it were said"," 'The Prophet (ﷺ) did not recite the *talbiyah* after the first pebble he threw","' and al-Fadl said"," 'He continued to recite *talbiyah* until he threw the seventh pebble","' then whoever understands knowledge"," excels in jurisprudence"," does not stubbornly oppose his intellect"," and does not deny what he knows"," will realize that the narration comes from the one who reports the occurrence or hears it"," not from the one who denies and rejects it.\n\nI have clarified this issue in various places in my books.\"""""
